"THE EKR PROPERTY FOR FLAG PURE (https://www.maths.usyd.edu.au/u/pubs/publist/preprints/2019/olarte-18.pdf)\nciteturn9search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Fomin and Zelevinsky [7], introduced the m-th generalized cluster complex of Φ as a ... Every generalized cluster complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR.\nthe number of vertices is of the form mn + 2. The sets of diagonals producing these\n(m+2)-angulations form a pure simplicial complex of dimension n−2, since any (m+2)-\nangulation has n −1 diagonals. More generally, for any ﬁnite root system Φ of rank\nn −1 and a positive integer m, Fomin and Reading [6], generalizing previous work of\nFomin and Zelevinsky [7], introduced the m-th generalized cluster complex of Φ as a\ncertain ﬂag pure complex C(m, Φ) of dimension n −2. They then showed that for root\nsystems of type An−1, the complex C(m, An−1) equals the complex of (m+2)-angulations\nof an (mn+2)-gon. Athanasiadis and Tzanaki [2] proved that the complexes C(m, Φ) are\nvertex-decomposable, hence homotopically equivalent to wedges of (n −2)-spheres, and\ngave a formula for the number of spheres in C(m, Φ).\nWe have computationally veriﬁed that for every n, m with n + m ≤8 and for every\nirreducible root system of rank n −1, the complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR, which leads to\nthe following conjectures.\nConjecture 1.5. The complex C(m, An−1) whose facets are the (m + 2)-angulations of\na convex (mn + 2)-gon is pure-EKR.\nConjecture 1.6. Every generalized cluster complex C(m, Φ) is pure-EKR.\nSumming up, we have the following diagram of implications:\nConj. 1.3\nConj. 1.4\nConj. 1.1\nConj. 1.2\nConj. 1.5\nConj. 1.6\nOur two main results are evidence for Conjecture 1.2. On the one hand, we prove the\nconjecture in dimensions up to three.\nTheorem 1.7. Pure, ﬂag simplicial complexes without boundary of dimension at most\nthree are pure-EKR.\nThis has the following immediate consequences.\nCorollary 1.8. Flag pseudo-manifolds, in particular simplicial manifolds, of dimension\nat most three are pure-EKR.\nCorollary 1.9. The generalized cluster complexes C(m, Φ), for arbitrary m, are pure-\nEKR for every Φ of rank at most four. In particular, the complex of (m + 2)-angulations\nof a convex (mn + 2)-gon is pure-EKR for m ≥1 and n ≤5.\nOn the other hand we prove that no intersecting family in which every member touches\na certain ﬁxed edge can have size larger than the largest star:\nTheorem 1.10. Let C be a ﬂag pure simplicial complex without boundary and let ab be\nan edge in C. Let F be an intersecting family of facets of C contained in the union of the\nstars of a and b. Then (at least) one of the two stars has size greater or equal than |F|.\nThe 2-dimensional case of Theorem 1.7 (see Theorem 2.16) is a corollary of the ma-\nchinery developed in Section 2 for proving Theorem 1.10. The 3-dimensional case (see\nTheorem 3.1) is more involved and uses additional machinery presented in Section 3.\nThe paper concludes in Section 4 with various constructions of examples of simplicial\ncomplexes which do not have the pure-EKR property.\n3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Mathematica® Journal (https://content.wolfram.com/sites/19/2013/12/Miyadera.pdf)\nciteturn9search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.9 years ago; Triangular chocolate games that satisfy the inequality y § Hx + zL ê k. ... This conjecture is Proposition 4 when k = 1 and is proved for k = 3 in [6]. ... the nim-sum part and k=1.\n18\nRyohei Miyadera, Shunsuke Nakamura, Yu Okada, Ryo Hanafusa, and Tomoki Ishikawa\ngame coordinates\nfirst player's turn\nx\n7\nz\n7\ny\n14\nk\n1\nnew game\nClick a line to make a move.\nÚ Figure 5. Triangular chocolate games that satisfy the inequality y § Hx + zL ê k.\nConjecture 1\nFor a triangular chocolate game satisfying y § Hx +zL ê k, 8x, y, z< is a losing state if\nand only if x Å⊕y Å⊕y = 0 when k = 3 (mod 4), and 8x +1, y, z +1< is a losing state if and\nonly if x Å⊕y Å⊕y = 0 when k = 1 (mod 4).\nThis conjecture is Proposition 4 when k = 1 and is proved for k = 3 in [6].\nFor other types of chocolate games that the authors studied, see [7], [6], [8], and [9].\nü Chocolate Games That Satisfy the Inequality y £ x +z\nManipulateA\nH* The code is the same as for Figure 5 except for\nthe nim-sum part and k=1. *L\nModuleA\n8ab, k = 1<,\nIf@y > Floor@Hx + zL ê kD, y = Floor@Hx + zL ê kDD;\nColumnA9\nIf@8x, y, z< == 80, 0, 0<,\nText@\nStyle@\nRow@8If@Mod@game5count, 2D == 0, \"second\", \"first\"D,\n\" player won\"<D, MediumDD,\nText@\nStyle@\nRow@8If@Mod@game5count, 2D == 0, \"first\", \"second\"D,\n\" player's turn\"<D, MediumDDD,\nEventHandler@\nThe Mathematica Journal 15 © 2013 Wolfram Media, Inc.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTop arXiv papers (https://scirate.com/?date=2026-03-30&page=115&range=7)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago;   * Graham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups ... Mar 24 2026 math.NT math.CO arXiv:2603.20961v1 ... @misc{2603.20961, author = {Simone Costa and Stefano Della Fiore and Mattia Fontana and Lluís Vena}, title = {{G}raham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2603.20961}, note = {arXiv:2603.20961v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n  * Graham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups\n\nSimone Costa, Stefano Della Fiore, Mattia Fontana, Lluís Vena\n\nMar 24 2026 math.NT math.CO arXiv:2603.20961v1\n\n[Button: Scited][Button: Scite!]\n\n0\n\n@misc{2603.20961, author = {Simone Costa and Stefano Della Fiore and Mattia Fontana and Lluís Vena}, title = {{G}raham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups}, year = {2026}, eprint = {2603.20961}, note = {arXiv:2603.20961v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nA famous conjecture of Graham asserts that every set $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{Z}_p \\setminus \\{0\\}$ can be ordered so that all partial sums are distinct. Although this conjecture was recently proved for sufficiently large primes by Pham and Sauermann in~\\citePM (combined with earlier results of \\citeBBKMM), it remains open for general abelian groups, even in the cyclic case $\\mathbb{Z}_k$. In this paper, using a recursive approach, we investigate the sequenceability of subsets $A$ in generic abelian groups for small values of $|A|$. We prove that any subset $A \\subseteq G\\setminus\\{0\\}$ with $|A| \\leq 20$ is sequenceable where previously it was known only for $|A|\\leq 9$. This bound is improved to $|A| \\leq 22$ for zero-sum subsets. Finally, regarding the related CMPP conjecture, we show that zero-sum subsets without inverse pairs are sequenceable for $|A| \\leq 23$.\n\n  * The Search for $K_L \\rightarrow \\pi^0\\pi^0\\gamma\\gamma$ and $K_L\\rightarrow \\pi^0\\pi^0X$ where $X\\rightarrow 2\\gamma$ at the KOTO Experiment\n\nJ. Redeker, C. Lin, Y. W. Wah, J. K. Ahn, M. Gonzalez, K. Hanai, Y. B. Hsiung, T. Kato, E. J. Kim, T. K. Komatsubara, K. Kotera, S. K. Lee, G. Y. Lim, T. Matsumura, H. Nanjo, T. Nomura, T. Nunes, K. Ono, K. Shiomi, R. Shiraishi, et al (6)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImpartial Triangular Chocolate Bar Games | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/1711.04954v1)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.0 years ago; Crawled: last month; Image: Triangular chocolate bar with coordinate system showing cutting lines and bitter triangle Figure 1: The triangular chocolate bar game setup, showing the coordinate system, cutting lines, and the bitter triangle (blue) that players must avoid. ... For k = 4m + 1 (such as k = 5), computational evidence suggests:\n\nConjecture: P-positions satisfy (x-1) ⊕ y ⊕ (z-1) = 0 ... Nakamura, Chocolate Games that are Variants of Nim, Mathematics of Puzzles, Journal of Information Processing, 53(6), (2012), 1582-1591 (in Japanese).\nSubmitted 14 Nov 2017\n\nen\n\n# Impartial Triangular Chocolate Bar Games\n\nKwansei Gakuin High SchoolImageOsaka University Hyogo University of Teacher Education\n\nRM\n\nRyohei Miyadera\n\nSN\n\nShunsuke Nakamura\n\nMF\n\nMasanori Fukui\n\n## Abstract\n\nChocolate bar games are variants of the game of Nim in which the goal is to leave your opponent with the single bitter part of the chocolate bar. The rectangular chocolate bar game is a thinly disguised form of classical multi-heap Nim. In this work, we investigate the mathematical structure of triangular chocolate bar games in which the triangular chocolate bar can be cut in three directions. In the triangular chocolate bar game, a position is a $\\mathcal{P}$P-position if and only if $x \\oplus y \\oplus z = 0$x⊕y⊕z=0, where the numbers $x,y,z$x,y,z stand for the maximum number of times that the chocolate bar can be cut in each direction. Moreover, the Grundy number of a position $(x,y,z)$(x,y,z) is not always equal to $x \\oplus y \\oplus z $, and a generic formula for Grundy numbers in not known. \nTheorem (P-position Characterization): For k = 4m + 3, a position (x, y, z) is a P-position (previous player wins) if and only if:\n\n  1. The geometric constraint y ≤ ⌊(x+z)/k⌋ is satisfied\n  2. The Nim-sum equals zero: x ⊕ y ⊕ z = 0\n\nThis result emerges from an intricate analysis involving sequences derived from the binary digits of x, y, and z, classified into three types based on recursive functions that track how the constraint y = ⌊(x+z)/k⌋ behaves under the game's move structure.\n\n## The Nim-Sum Paradox\n\nA crucial finding reveals a subtle but important distinction between this triangular game and classical Nim. While P-positions are characterized by x ⊕ y ⊕ z = 0 (similar to Nim), the individual Grundy numbers G((x,y,z)) do not always equal x ⊕ y ⊕ z.\n\nFor example, with k = 3, the position (1,1,2) has:\n\n  * Nim-sum: 1 ⊕ 1 ⊕ 2 = 2\n  * Grundy number: G((1,1,2)) = 4\n\nThis demonstrates that while the game exhibits \"Nim-like\" behavior in its P-position structure, it is not equivalent to a simple combination of three Nim piles. The geometric constraints create a more complex strategic landscape where the Sprague-Grundy values deviate from the simple Nim-sum formula.\n\n## Computational Exploration and Conjectures\n\nThe research extends beyond the proven case of k = 4m + 3 through computational investigation. For k = 4m + 1 (such as k = 5), computational evidence suggests:\n\nConjecture: P-positions satisfy (x-1) ⊕ y ⊕ (z-1) = 0\n\nThis indicates a \"shifted\" Nim-sum formula, where the effective coordinates for the XOR calculation are modified. However, for even values of k, the computational results show irregular patterns with no apparent simple formula, suggesting fundamentally different mathematical behavior.\n\nThe computational approach uses both Mathematica and the Combinatorial Game Suite (CGSuite) to verify theoretical results and explore parameter ranges beyond analytical tractability. This hybrid methodology demonstrates the power of combining rigorous mathematical proof with computational exploration in modern game theory research.\n\n## Significance and Broader Impact\n\nThis work contributes several important insights to combinatorial game theory. First, it expands the catalog of well-understood impartial games by introducing geometric complexity while maintaining analytical tractability for specific parameter values. Second, it demonstrates how seemingly minor changes in game geometry can preserve some Nim-like properties while breaking others, providing nuanced understanding of what makes games \"Nim-equivalent.\"\n\nThe research methodology, involving sophisticated sequence analysis and binary representation techniques, offers tools that could be applicable to other geometrically constrained games. The explicit computational conjectures for different parameter values provide clear directions for future theoretical work.\n\nFrom a pedagogical perspective, the triangular chocolate bar game serves as an excellent example of how intuitive game concepts can hide mathematical depth. The interplay between geometric constraints and strategic analysis makes it suitable for demonstrating advanced concepts in combinatorial game theory while remaining conceptually accessible.\n\nThe work also highlights the growing importance of computational methods in game theory research, where theoretical analysis and computer verification work together to push the boundaries of understanding in complex game systems.\n\nA poisoned chocolate problem\n\nThis citation is essential as it introduced the original chocolate bar game. The paper being analyzed frames its own triangular game as a variant of the classic rectangular game described by Robin, making this the foundational problem.\n\n[1] A.C. Robin, A poisoned chocolate problem, Problem corner, The Mathematical Gazette, 73, (1989), 341-343. The answer for this problem can be found in 74, (1990), 171-173.\n\nNim, a game with a complete mathematical theory\n\nThis is a foundational paper on the game of Nim, whose mathematical structure (specifically the nim-sum) is central to the analysis in the main paper. The authors repeatedly contrast their triangular game with the rectangular version, which is mathematically equivalent to Nim.\n\n[2] C. L. Bouton, Nim, a game with a complete mathematical theory, Annals of Mathematics, 3(14), (1901), 35.\n\nChocolate Games that are Variants of Nim\n\nThis citation is highly relevant because the current paper explicitly states that it generalizes the results from this prior work, which studied a simpler type of triangular chocolate bar game. This makes it the direct predecessor to the research presented.\n\n[5] R. Miyadera, T. Inoue, W. Ogasa, and S. Nakamura, Chocolate Games that are Variants of Nim, Mathematics of Puzzles, Journal of Information Processing, 53(6), (2012), 1582-1591 (in Japanese).\n\nLessons In Play\n\nThis textbook is cited as a primary reference for the fundamental concepts of combinatorial game theory used throughout the paper. It provides the theoretical background for P-positions, N-positions, and the Sprague–Grundy theory, which are the core analytical tools employed.\n\n[7] M.H. Albert, R.J. Nowakowski, and D. Wolfe, Lessons In Play, A K Peters/CRC Press, (2007).\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{miyadera2017impartialtriangularchocolatebar, title={Impartial Triangular Chocolate Bar Games}, author={Ryohei Miyadera and Shunsuke Nakamura and Masanori Fukui}, year={2017}, eprint={1711.04954}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.04954}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRussell Jay Hendel - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/russell-jay-hendel)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; 2026-01-07 ... In Evan and Hendel's recent proof of an outstanding conjecture on the resistance distances of a family of linear 3-trees, a key technique in the proof was calculating the recursion satisfied by a family of determinants. The underlying algorithm employed to prove the conjecture converged (i.e. terminated) in the particular case studied, and the paper presented an open question on when such a procedure converges in general. ... Barret, Evans, and Francis conjectured that if $G$ is the straight linear 3-tree with $n$ vertices and $H$ is the straight linear 3-tree with $n+1$ vertices then \\[\\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty} r_{H} (1, n+1) - r_G(1,n) = \\frac{1}{14},\\] where $r_G(u,v)$ and $r_H(u,v)$ are the resistance distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in graphs $G$ and $H$ respectively.\n# arXiv++ Combinatorics\n\nBrowse math.CO papers from arXiv\n\n[Input: Search by title, author, BibTeX key, or arXiv URL...] [Button: Search]\n\n## Papers by Russell Jay Hendel\n\n6 paper(s) by this author · All BibTeX\n\n2026-01-07\n\nRussell Jay Hendel\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nProof of Convergence of a Laplace Expansion Algorithm For Calculating Recursions Satisfied by a Family of Determinants\n\nIn Evan and Hendel's recent proof of an outstanding conjecture on the resistance distances of a family of linear 3-trees, a key technique in the proof was calculating the recursion satisfied by a family of determinants. The underlying algorithm employed to prove the conjecture converged (i.e. terminated) in the particular case studied, and the paper presented an open question on when such a procedure converges in general. This paper proves convergence of the procedure for an arbitrary family of determinants of banded, square, Toeplitz matrices. A variety of methods are used in the field to determine resistance distance including recursive, mathematical, and graphical techniques. Sequences familiar to the readers of the Fibonacci Quarterly such as the Fibonacci and Lucas sequences appear quite often in results in the literature. Twenty five to forty years ago there were a handful of papers on resistance that appeared in the Fibonacci Quarterly and the Proceedings and recently papers on the subject have appeared again. It is hoped that this introductory expository account will interest readers of the Quarterly to renew interest in this current and active field.\n\ncircuit graph lucas sequence sequence variety\n\n2025-05-26\n\nEmily J. Evans, Russell Jay Hendel\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nProof of a Conjecture on the Growth of the Maximal Resistance Distance in a Linear 3--Tree\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nBarret, Evans, and Francis conjectured that if $G$ is the straight linear 3-tree with $n$ vertices and $H$ is the straight linear 3-tree with $n+1$ vertices then \\[\\lim_{n\\rightarrow \\infty} r_{H} (1, n+1) - r_G(1,n) = \\frac{1}{14},\\] where $r_G(u,v)$ and $r_H(u,v)$ are the resistance distance between vertices $u$ and $v$ in graphs $G$ and $H$ respectively. In this paper, we prove the conjecture by looking at the determinants of deleted Laplacian matrices. The proof uses a Laplace expansion method on a family of determinants to determine the underlying recursion this family satisfies and then uses routine linear algebra methods to obtain an exact Binet formula for the $n$-th term.\n\ndeterminant graph laplacian matrix tree\n\n2022-05-08\n\nRussell Jay Hendel\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA System of Four simultaneous Recursions: Generalization of the Ledin-Shannon-Ollerton Identity\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGraham conjecture on small sets in abelian groups — Plain-Language Explanation | Gist.Science (https://gist.science/paper/2603.20961)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; In summary, the paper proves that the Graham Conjecture holds for all subsets of size up to 20 in any abelian group, and up to 23 under specific structural constraints (zero-sum, no inverse pairs), utilizing a novel recursive reduction strategy backed by rigorous computational verification. ... 2603.20961\n#### Technical Details\n\n  * The authors verified that for $k \\le 23$k≤23, the recursive reduction always succeeds or a contradiction is found computationally.\n  * They explicitly calculated coefficients for the Combinatorial Nullstellensatz polynomial for $k \\in [10, 23]$k∈[10,23] (Table 1), confirming the polynomial method works for $\\mathbb{Z}_p$Z p​ in this range.\n  * The computational search confirmed that no counterexamples exist for these sizes in generic abelian groups.\n\n### 4. Significance\n\n  * Resolution of Small Cases: This work closes the gap for small sets in generic abelian groups, a domain where previous results were limited to size 9.\n  * Methodological Innovation: The combination of the Recursive Lemma (reducing the problem size) with a constraint-based BFS search (verifying the base cases) provides a powerful framework. The authors note that without the Recursive Lemma, the pure tree-algorithm would fail at size 10, consistent with prior literature.\n  * Computational Feasibility: The paper demonstrates that sequenceability problems, often considered intractable for large $N$N, can be solved for specific structural constraints (small $k$k) using modern computational algebra and parallel processing.\n  * Foundation for Future Work: By establishing the sequenceability of sets up to size 23, the paper provides a robust base for further inductive proofs or for tackling the general conjecture for larger sets in specific group structures.\n\nIn summary, the paper proves that the Graham Conjecture holds for all subsets of size up to 20 in any abelian group, and up to 23 under specific structural constraints (zero-sum, no inverse pairs), utilizing a novel recursive reduction strategy backed by rigorous computational verification.\n\nSource paper\n\narXiv\n\n2603.20961\n\nView on arXiv ↗PDF ↗--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe EKR property for flag pure simplicial complexes without boundary - MaRDI portal (https://portal.mardi4nfdi.de/wiki/Publication%3A2299647)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; simplicial complexes cluster complexes EKR flag (pseudo-)manifolds Erdős-Ko-Rado property ...   * Shellability and higher Cohen-Macaulay connectivity of generalized cluster complexes\n  * Short proof of two cases of Chvátal's conjecture\n  * scientific article; zbMATH DE number 3477225 (Why is no real title available?)\n  * scientific article; zbMATH DE number 3621717 (Why is no real title available?)\n  * scientific article; zbMATH DE number 2246193 (Why is no real title available?)\n  * Cluster algebras. II: Finite type classification\n  * Compression and Erdős-Ko-Rado graphs\n  * Erdős-Ko-Rado theorems for simplicial complexes\n  * Erdős-Ko-Rado theorems. Algebraic approaches\n  * Extremal t -intersecting sub-families of hereditary families\n  * Graphs with the Erdős-Ko-Rado property\n  * INTERSECTION THEOREMS FOR SYSTEMS OF FINITE SETS\n